{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790264005,"nonce":"Ys3etb5IgAgB5NGhPJPvj7DAv1s=","title":"Python: an optional parameter no call site passes could be a local initialised from its default","message":"A default parameter value that no call site omits no longer widens the parameter (5ffcc5f5). The other half is still open: a parameter with a default that no call site ever passes is still a parameter, so every call passes the default value and the callee cannot treat it as a constant. It could instead be a local initialised from the default on entry, and dropped from the signature. This applies only when every call site is known (no escape as a value, no *args/**kwargs forwarding) and the default is evaluated once, at definition time, as Python requires: a mutable default shared across calls must stay one object.","files":null}]}